<p>Summarize how the 10-fold increase in the sample size has changed your conclusions. Do you have stronger evidence that yawning is contagious? Is the evidence now very strong that yawning is contagious?</p>
</statement>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Relative risk</title>
<p>A previous section introduced you to the relative risk as an alternative statistic to summarize the relationship between two binary variables.</p>
<activity>
 <!--Question 35-->
<statement>
<p>What value of the relative risk indicates no association between the two variables in this study?</p>
</statement>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 36-->
<statement>
<p>State the null and alternative hypotheses for the yawning study in terms of the relative risk.</p>
</statement>
</activity>

<p>We will now apply the 3S strategy using the relative risk. Use the sample size of 500 people you used in questions 32-34 for questions 37 and 38.</p>
<activity>
 <!--Question 37-->
<statement>
<p>Statistic. Calculate, and then interpret, the observed value of the relative risk by dividing the conditional proportion of yawners in the yawn seed group compared to the conditional proportion of yawners in the no-yawn seed group.</p>
</statement>
</activity>

<p>In the <url href="http://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/chisqshuffle/ChiSqShuffle.htm?yawning=1&amp;hideExtras=2"> Two Proportions applet</url>, change the statistic to "Relative risk" in the Statistic pull-down menu. Confirm that the statistic matches your answer to question 37.</p>
<activity>
 <!--Question 38-->
<statement>
<p>Simulate. If you still have your shuffles from question 33 use them; otherwise generate 1,000 new shuffles. Find the p-value using the relative risk. Compare this p-value to the p-value you obtained in question 33.</p>
</statement>
</activity>

<p>If you used the same simulated tables, you should have found that the p-value for the relative risk is exactly the same as for the difference in proportions! This is because there is a "one-to-one" correspondence between these statistics.</p>
</subsection>

</section>